1 Rationale
***********

Running binaries in containers is useful for users of immutable distros
(e.g.  Fedora Silverblue) who cannot conveniently (or simply do not want
to) install toolchains on their host system, and more generally for
anyone wanting to declaratively define their project's build
environment.

   GNOME Builder (https://apps.gnome.org/Builder/) nails this, and is
the target whose UX this package tries to mimic: it lets you define a
per-project build environment, with compilation commands and LSP
requests redirected to it, while git handling and spell checking stay on
the host as "editor-coupled" tools.

   Emacs provides TRAMP out-of-the-box, an incredibly versatile facility
for transparently accessing remote environments including containers.
However, its usage semantics fall short here: container files are
treated as distinct from their host counterparts despite being the same
file, forcing you to switch buffers or jump between ‘/foo.c’ and
‘/toolbox:MyToolbox:/foo.c’.  Also, executable access is all-or-nothing,
when visiting a container's buffers, every needed tool must be installed
there


