3.1 Setting the environment
===========================

To use runbox, ‘runbox-environment’ must be set and ‘default-directory’
(current directory) must be under ‘runbox-bind-mount’.

 -- User Option: runbox-environment
     the target environment for Runbox commands.

     If set, it takes a ‘tramp-file-name’ struct as obtained by
     ‘(tramp-dissect-file-name)’.  Its ‘localname’ slot is ignored.

     It can be set interactively with a string provided input through
     _Customize_ facilties:
        • ‘customize-set-value’ to set it buffer-locally
        • ‘customize-set-variable’ to set it globally
        • ‘customize-dirlocals’ or ‘project-customize-dirlocals’ to set
          it scoped to a directory.
             • May be unsafe as a file-local variable.  See section
               "*note Runbox-environment as a safe file-local variable:
               Runbox-environment as a safe file-local variable.".

Constant: ‘runbox-bind-mount’
     Local host path bind-mounted into the routed environment.  Defaults
     to the host user's home directory.  May be declared as a variable
     in the future.

   When setting the environment ensure that your TRAMP method of choice
is enabled.  Toolbox is provided out of the box in Emacs 30, but it has
to be manually enabled:

     M-x tramp-enable-method toolbox

   When ‘runbox-environment’ is correctly set and ‘default-directory’
points to a valid path, an indicator in the mode-line is shown and
runbox commands can be used.  One such example is ‘runbox-shell’ which
runs ‘shell’ in the context of the specified environment.  Other
commands are listed in "*note Runbox dispatcher commands/functions":
Runbox dispatcher commands/functions. section.


