3.2 Defining a leader key
=========================

It is possible define a leader key so that any key sequence issued right
after will spawn its associated runbox equivalent command, if any.  To
do so, bind the desired key-sequence for the leader key to
‘runbox-any-command’.

 -- Command: runbox-any-command
     Reads the next key sequence and runs the runbox-equivalent of the
     command bound to it, if any.

   Such is an example that sets ‘C-z’ as a leader key and ‘C-x t’ for
shell:

     (bind-keys ("C-z" . runbox-any-command)
                ("C-x t" . shell))

   With the above example, issuing ‘C-z C-x t’ will invoke the
‘runbox-shell’ command.

   Please note: ‘runbox-any-command’ only works for event from the
top-level command event loop: doing ‘C-z M-x shell’ won't work as
regular ‘shell’ command will be invoked.


File: runbox.info,  Node: Enabling automatic command dispatch,  Next: Runbox dispatcher commands/functions,  Prev: Defining a leader key,  Up: Usage

3.3 Enabling automatic command dispatch
=======================================

Instead of a leader key, you can modally choose to replace every issued
command with their runbox counterparts, if any:

Minor mode: ‘runbox-auto-mode’
     when non-nil, replaces the issued commands with their runbox
     counterpart.

     The replacement occurs only when ‘runbox-environment’ is set and
     valid (See *note Setting the environment: Setting the environment.
     section)

Global minor mode: ‘global-runbox-auto-mode’

   Just like with the leader key approach explained before, this suffers
of the same shortcomings: only the command issued from top-level command
loop are replaced.  Typing ‘M-x shell’ will still open a regular local
shell.


