4.1 Customizing the mode-line
=============================

By default a mode-line-construct is displayed in the
‘mode-line-misc-info’ area when Runbox commands can be executed (see
*note Setting the environment: Setting the environment. section).  Such
mode-line construct's format is defined by the ‘runbox-mode-line’
variable.

‘‘runbox-mode-line’’
     Variable for the user facing mode-line construct for Runbox, to be
     added in the mode-line.

   This construct has many nested constructs that allow for granular
customization.  Refer to its docstring.


File: runbox.info,  Node: Labeling Runbox environments,  Next: Runbox-environment as a safe file-local variable,  Prev: Customizing the mode-line,  Up: Customizing

4.2 Labeling Runbox environments
================================

By default, the mode-line shows the currently set ‘runbox-environment’.
As ‘runbox-environment’ holds a ‘tramp-file-name’ struct, evaluating a
human readable string for it is necessary.

User-option: ‘runbox-environment-label-formatters’
     Alist of ‘(PREDICATE-FN . FORMATTER-FN)’.  Both are functions of
     one argument, a ‘tramp-file-name’ vector.  The first entry whose
     ‘PREDICATE-FN’ returns non-nil has its ‘FORMATTER-FN’ called to
     produce the label.

 -- Function: runbox-environment-label environment
     Return a string with a human readable name for environment.
     Consults ‘runbox-environment-label-formatters’, as a fallback, it
     returns a string constructed with ‘tramp-make-tramp-name’ minus its
     localname and its leading ‘/’ and trailing ‘:’ (for example
     ‘podman:Container’)

 -- Function: runbox-environment-label-cached environment
     Like ‘runbox-environment-label’, but result is cached by
     ‘environment’'s identity.  Useful for hot loops and mode line
     rendering.


File: runbox.info,  Node: Runbox-environment as a safe file-local variable,  Prev: Labeling Runbox environments,  Up: Customizing

4.3 ‘Runbox-environment’ as a safe file-local variable
======================================================

By default ‘runbox-environment’ value is safe if it is nil, if it has a
safe method according to ‘runbox-safe-methods’ or if it is a safe
‘tramp-file-name’ according to ‘runbox-environment-p-functions’.

‘‘runbox-safe-methods’ ‘'("toolbox" "distrobox")’’
     A list of string with TRAMP methods deemed safe if set as
     file-local.

‘‘runbox-safe-environment-p-functions’’
     A list of 1-arg functions that take a ‘tramp-file-name’ struct as a
     parameter and return non-nil if it is safe if set in a file-local
     variable.

   • Function: ‘runbox-safe-environment-p environment’ Returns non-nil
     if ‘environment’ is safe if set in a file-local variable.


